<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Cleartext HTTP for the tutorial's lab network.

    Android has blocked cleartext by default since API 28, and the failure
    is an opaque IOException that names nothing — the reader sees a
    request that never leaves the phone with no indication that the
    platform, not the network, refused it.

    ## Why this is now a blanket permission for debug

    The previous version listed private ranges as <domain> entries:

        <domain includeSubdomains="true">172.16.0.0</domain>

    <domain> matches a hostname suffix. It is not CIDR. So `172.16.0.0`
    permitted the literal address `172.16.0.0` and nothing else —
    `172.30.1.67`, the machine the reader actually runs `npm start` on,
    was blocked. Emulator and localhost worked, every real handset did
    not, and the symptom was a button that flickered and did nothing.

    Android offers no way to express "any RFC1918 address" here. The
    honest choice for a tutorial app is to permit cleartext outright and
    say so, rather than ship a list that looks like a restriction and is
    not one.

    This is a demo app for a lab network. A product would use TLS and
    delete this file.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
